--TEST--
`Core\Cache\SecretEntry::of` answers one object carrying both halves a fetch learned, built fresh at every call, with each of its two parameters callable by name — `rule:core-api/shape-rules`, `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R14: anything with a lifetime is an object, so a
// fill hands back one value rather than a secret and a number a caller could
// pair up wrongly. R16 is why the class is named as a singular noun.
secret string $token = "hunter2";
Core\Cache\SecretEntry $one = Core\Cache\SecretEntry::of($token, 30s);
echo "of answers an entry: ", Core\Reflect::typeOf($one) == Core\Reflect\TypeKind::Object ? "object" : "other", "\n";

// Two calls written identically are two entries. A fill builds one each time
// it fetches, so nothing here is interned against the arguments and no entry
// is shared with the one a previous fetch made.
Core\Cache\SecretEntry $two = Core\Cache\SecretEntry::of($token, 30s);
echo "two calls, two entries: ", $one == $two ? "same" : "distinct", "\n";

// The other side of that bound: one entry under two names is one entry, so the
// line above reports what the calls did rather than how `==` reads an object
// at all.
var $again = $one;
echo "one entry, two names: ", $one == $again ? "same" : "distinct", "\n";

// R2: every parameter is callable by name, this member's two included.
Core\Cache\SecretEntry $named = Core\Cache\SecretEntry::of(value: $token, ttl: 30s);
echo "by name: ", Core\Reflect::typeOf($named) == Core\Reflect\TypeKind::Object ? "object" : "other", "\n";
--EXPECT--
of answers an entry: object
two calls, two entries: distinct
one entry, two names: same
by name: object
